            "summary": "(Automatic external link checking: updating and/or deleting links that are broken)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Hold your baby in a semi-upright position and support their head. When it’s time to feed your baby, sit in a comfortable chair and hold them with their head in the crook of your arm and your hand down around their bottom. Their head should be comfortably above their belly. This is called the cradle position<ref>https://www.parents.com/baby/care/american-baby-how-tos/best-positions-to-bottle-feed-baby/</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 8 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Set the bottle down and try to burp every {{convert|2|-|3|USoz|mL|abbr=on}} of consumed milk. Bottle-fed infants suck a lot more air during a feed than infants eating from a breast, so it’s important to burp them frequently during a feed. Burping them throughout the feed makes them more comfortable and more likely to finish their bottle.<ref>https://www.parents.com/baby/care/american-baby-how-tos/best-positions-to-bottle-feed-baby/</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 16.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Drape your baby over your shoulder. To burp your newborn, hold them with their head high up and resting over your shoulder, supporting their head the whole time. Holding them this high places slight pressure on their tummy and eases the air out through a burp.<ref>https://www.parents.com/baby/care/american-baby-how-tos/best-positions-to-bottle-feed-baby/</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 17.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Firmly pat or rub their back. To help your baby’s burp come out, put some pressure on their back by patting or rubbing it. Your baby will be calmed by the repetitive patting or rubbing, and more likely to burp.<ref>https://www.parents.com/baby/care/american-baby-how-tos/best-positions-to-bottle-feed-baby/</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 18.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Resume feeding after a couple minutes even if your baby doesn’t burp. If your baby hasn’t finished their bottle, you can try feeding them again after about 2 minutes of trying to burp them whether they actually burp or not. If they are not interested in eating more but seem fussy, you can try burping them again or check to see if they need a diaper change. If they seem content but uninterested in eating, they are probably full and have finished eating for this feed.<ref>https://www.parents.com/baby/care/american-baby-how-tos/best-positions-to-bottle-feed-baby/</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 19.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Hold your baby in a semi-upright position and support their head. When it’s time to feed your baby, sit in a comfortable chair and hold them with their head in the crook of your arm and your hand down around their bottom. Their head should be comfortably above their belly. This is called the cradle position<ref>https://www.parents.com/baby/feeding/bottlefeeding/newborn-basics-bottlefeeding/</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 8 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Set the bottle down and try to burp every {{convert|2|-|3|USoz|mL|abbr=on}} of consumed milk. Bottle-fed infants suck a lot more air during a feed than infants eating from a breast, so it’s important to burp them frequently during a feed. Burping them throughout the feed makes them more comfortable and more likely to finish their bottle.<ref>https://www.parents.com/baby/feeding/bottlefeeding/newborn-basics-bottlefeeding/</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 16.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Drape your baby over your shoulder. To burp your newborn, hold them with their head high up and resting over your shoulder, supporting their head the whole time. Holding them this high places slight pressure on their tummy and eases the air out through a burp.<ref>https://www.parents.com/baby/feeding/bottlefeeding/newborn-basics-bottlefeeding/</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 17.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Firmly pat or rub their back. To help your baby’s burp come out, put some pressure on their back by patting or rubbing it. Your baby will be calmed by the repetitive patting or rubbing, and more likely to burp.<ref>https://www.parents.com/baby/feeding/bottlefeeding/newborn-basics-bottlefeeding/</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 18.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Resume feeding after a couple minutes even if your baby doesn’t burp. If your baby hasn’t finished their bottle, you can try feeding them again after about 2 minutes of trying to burp them whether they actually burp or not. If they are not interested in eating more but seem fussy, you can try burping them again or check to see if they need a diaper change. If they seem content but uninterested in eating, they are probably full and have finished eating for this feed.<ref>https://www.parents.com/baby/feeding/bottlefeeding/newborn-basics-bottlefeeding/</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 19.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
