        {
            "id": "32666821",
            "user": "WikiHow Horizon",
            "timestamp": "16:36, 21 July 2024",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(15,538 bytes)",
            "change": 175,
            "summary": "(Updated link/s)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Place the nipple against your baby’s lower lip to prompt them to open their mouth. If your baby is hungry, they will open their mouths to accept something that’s placed on their lower lip. Gently place the bottle nipple against their lower lip.<ref>https://www.nct.org.uk/baby-toddler/feeding/practical-tips/tips-for-bottle-feeding-your-baby</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 9.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Tilt the bottle to keep the nipple full of milk or formula. As you feed your baby, you want the nipple to stay full so that your baby doesn’t suck air. Keep the bottle tilted with the bottom pointed up while it’s in your baby’s mouth.<ref>https://www.nct.org.uk/baby-toddler/feeding/practical-tips/tips-for-bottle-feeding-your-baby</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 10.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Allow your baby to draw the nipple into their mouth and suck. As you bring the nipple up into your baby’s open mouth, they will latch onto it and start to suck and swallow. This is their natural response when they are hungry. When they start to slow their sucking and swallowing, bring the nipple partially out of their mouth and allow them to draw it back in again.<ref>https://www.nct.org.uk/baby-toddler/feeding/practical-tips/tips-for-bottle-feeding-your-baby</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 11.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Look into your baby’s eyes and cuddle them or talk to them while feeding. To add to the feeding experience and bond with your baby, you can talk to them, cuddle them, and look into their eyes while they drink. They will look forward to feeding moments not only to satisfy their hunger, but as a way to spend time feeling loved by you.<ref>https://www.nct.org.uk/baby-toddler/feeding/practical-tips/tips-for-bottle-feeding-your-baby</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 12.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Follow your baby’s lead for how much and how often to feed them. Little babies differ from one another in how much and how often they need to eat. In general, a formula-fed newborn will eat {{convert|2|-|4|USoz|mL|abbr=on}} of formula 6-8 times every 24 hours up until 2 months old. Babies who drink expressed breastmilk may need to eat more often, as breastmilk is metabolized faster than formula.<ref>https://www.nct.org.uk/baby-toddler/feeding/practical-tips/tips-for-bottle-feeding-your-baby</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 13.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Place the nipple against your baby’s lower lip to prompt them to open their mouth. If your baby is hungry, they will open their mouths to accept something that’s placed on their lower lip. Gently place the bottle nipple against their lower lip.<ref>https://www.nct.org.uk/parenting/bottle-feeding-your-baby</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 9.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Tilt the bottle to keep the nipple full of milk or formula. As you feed your baby, you want the nipple to stay full so that your baby doesn’t suck air. Keep the bottle tilted with the bottom pointed up while it’s in your baby’s mouth.<ref>https://www.nct.org.uk/parenting/bottle-feeding-your-baby</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 10.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Allow your baby to draw the nipple into their mouth and suck. As you bring the nipple up into your baby’s open mouth, they will latch onto it and start to suck and swallow. This is their natural response when they are hungry. When they start to slow their sucking and swallowing, bring the nipple partially out of their mouth and allow them to draw it back in again.<ref>https://www.nct.org.uk/parenting/bottle-feeding-your-baby</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 11.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Look into your baby’s eyes and cuddle them or talk to them while feeding. To add to the feeding experience and bond with your baby, you can talk to them, cuddle them, and look into their eyes while they drink. They will look forward to feeding moments not only to satisfy their hunger, but as a way to spend time feeling loved by you.<ref>https://www.nct.org.uk/parenting/bottle-feeding-your-baby</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 12.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Follow your baby’s lead for how much and how often to feed them. Little babies differ from one another in how much and how often they need to eat. In general, a formula-fed newborn will eat {{convert|2|-|4|USoz|mL|abbr=on}} of formula 6-8 times every 24 hours up until 2 months old. Babies who drink expressed breastmilk may need to eat more often, as breastmilk is metabolized faster than formula.<ref>https://www.nct.org.uk/parenting/bottle-feeding-your-baby</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 13.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
