            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Start with a slow-flow nipple when your baby is very small.<ref>[v161458_b01]. 5 May 2020.</ref> Very young newborns in the first days are more easily able to choke when they’re eating. Start with the lowest number level of nipple available with the bottles you have. As your baby gets older, you can increase the flow speed to suit their preference.<ref>https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/pregnancy-and-baby/bottle-feeding-advice/</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 1 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#[[Sterilize Bottles|Sterilize]] your bottle and nipple daily. There are several ways to sterilize bottles, but you can submerge your bottle, nipple, and any other pieces in boiling water for 5 minutes as a simple method. You can also use the sanitize setting on your dishwasher.<ref>[v161458_b01]. 5 May 2020.</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 2 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Check the manufacturer’s label about the safety of sterilizing plastic bottles. Some plastics contain chemicals that should not be exposed to high heat. Many parents choose glass bottles for this reason.<ref>[v161458_b01]. 5 May 2020.</ref>",
                    "#Feed your baby when they show hunger cues. Newborns show that they are hungry by moving their heads from side to side, opening their mouths, sticking out their tongues, sucking on their fingers or fists, or trying to suck on anything that comes near their cheek. Crying is actually a late hunger cue, and it’s best to try to feed your baby before they are crying because it’s easier to calm them down to eat.<ref>[v161458_b01]. 5 May 2020.</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 7 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Don’t worry if your baby doesn’t want to finish a bottle, or if they want more after they finish a bottle. Sometimes they’ll want to eat less, and sometimes they’ll have a growth spurt and need more.<ref>[v161458_b01]. 5 May 2020.</ref> The suggested feeding amount is a general guideline, and if they are drinking between {{convert|12|-|32|USoz|mL|abbr=on}} of milk within 24 hours, they are eating enough.",
                    "",
                    ""
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Start with a slow-flow nipple when your baby is very small. Very young newborns in the first days are more easily able to choke when they’re eating. Start with the lowest number level of nipple available with the bottles you have. As your baby gets older, you can increase the flow speed to suit their preference.<ref>https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/pregnancy-and-baby/bottle-feeding-advice/</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 1 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#[[Sterilize Bottles|Sterilize]] your bottle and nipple daily. There are several ways to sterilize bottles, but you can submerge your bottle, nipple, and any other pieces in boiling water for 5 minutes as a simple method. You can also use the sanitize setting on your dishwasher.<ref>https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/pregnancy-and-baby/bottle-feeding-advice/</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 2 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Check the manufacturer’s label about the safety of sterilizing plastic bottles. Some plastics contain chemicals that should not be exposed to high heat. Many parents choose glass bottles for this reason.",
                    "#Feed your baby when they show hunger cues. Newborns show that they are hungry by moving their heads from side to side, opening their mouths, sticking out their tongues, sucking on their fingers or fists, or trying to suck on anything that comes near their cheek. Crying is actually a late hunger cue, and it’s best to try to feed your baby before they are crying because it’s easier to calm them down to eat.<ref>https://kidshealth.org/en/parents/formulafeed-often.html</ref>[[Image:Bottle Feed a Newborn Step 7 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Don’t worry if your baby doesn’t want to finish a bottle, or if they want more after they finish a bottle. Sometimes they’ll want to eat less, and sometimes they’ll have a growth spurt and need more. The suggested feeding amount is a general guideline, and if they are drinking between {{convert|12|-|32|USoz|mL|abbr=on}} of milk within 24 hours, they are eating enough."
                ]
            }
        },
