            "id": "9563118",
            "user": "Microsoft Sam",
            "timestamp": "04:23, 6 April 2013",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(5,245 bytes)",
            "change": 100,
            "summary": "(→‎Steps)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Hold your baby the right way. You'll have to observe your baby to get a sense of whether or not he or she is comfortable. If you hear a lot of loud sucking noises when he or she is drinking, then he or she may be taking in too much air. To help your baby take in less air, hold him or her at a 45-degree angle. Cradle him or her in a semi-upright position and support his or her head.",
                    "#*Tilt the bottle so the nipple and neck are always filled with milk or formula.",
                    "#*''Don't feed your baby when he or she is lying down.'' He or she can get an ear infection if the formula flows in his or her middle ear.",
                    "#*Until your baby weights about ten pounds, he or she will typically eat one to three ounces of formula during each feeding.",
                    "#*Don't force your baby to eat when he or she isn't hungry, and don't force him or her to finish the bottle if he or she doesn't want to. As long as your baby still shows interest in feeding from time to time, there's no need to force it.",
                    "#*If your baby continues to suck on the bottle when there's nothing left, that means he or she is hungry. Give him a little bit more to eat.",
                    "#Throw out any leftover formula. Don't put away the formula and use it later. Used formula should not be reused because of potential bacteria growth."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Hold your baby the right way. You'll have to observe your baby to get a sense of whether or not he's comfortable. If you hear a lot of loud sucking noises when he's drinking, then he may be taking in too much air. To help your baby take in less air, hold him at a 45-degree angle. Cradle him in a semi-upright position and support his head.",
                    "#*Tilt the bottle so the nipple and neck are always filled with milk or formula.",
                    "#*''Don't feed your baby when he's lying down.'' He can get an ear infection if the formula flows in his middle ear.",
                    "#*Until your baby weights about ten pounds, he will typically eat one to three ounces of formula during each feeding.",
                    "#*Don't force your baby to eat when he isn't hungry, and don't force him to finish the bottle if he doesn't want to. As long as your baby still shows interest in feeding from time to time, there's no need to force it.",
                    "#*If your baby continues to suck on the bottle when there's nothing left, that means he's hungry. Give him a little bit more to eat.",
                    "#Throw out any leftover formula. Don't put away the formula and use it later. Used formula should not be reused because of potential bacteria growth."
                ]
            }
        },
